docs.text:
Programs must be written for people to read, and only incidentally for machines to execute. — Harold Abelson and Gerald Jay Sussman, Structure and Interpretation of Computer Programs
Every Scala codebase carries a second program, written in prose. It sits in the doc comments above classes and methods, and it explains what the code means: what a field holds, what a parameter is for, why an exception gets thrown. The compiler reads it once, mostly to throw it away, and scaladoc turns it into a web site. Then it rests.
That’s a waste, because the prose is often exactly what other tools are missing. A JSON Schema derived from a case class can say that a field is a string, but not that it’s the name shown to other users. An OpenAPI spec knows the shape of a request, not its purpose. The purpose is right there in the source, one line above the shape.
This library puts that prose to work. It has five parts:
a model of what a doc comment says: its tags (@param, @return, …) and its markup (paragraphs, headings, lists, tables, code, links, bold and the rest) a parser that turns comment text into the model. It reads scaladoc’s wiki markup and Markdown, as Scala 3’s scaladoc does, with HTML and javadoc’s inline tags, on the JVM and on Scala.js Scaladoc.of[T], which hands you the scaladoc of a type at compile time, in macros and type class derivation Typedoc.of[T], which hands you the scaladoc of a sealed hierarchy or an enum with its variants’, and of a case class with its fields’, as a tree a compiler plugin (Scala 2) that embeds the scaladoc of classes into their bytecode, so it survives the trip into a jar
Here is the whole idea in a few lines. A case class and a sealed hierarchy document themselves, and the code asks them what they said:
import scaladoc._

object model {

  /** A user of the app.
    *
    * Users sign up with an e-mail address and can change their name later.
    *
    * @param name the name shown to other users
    * @param age  how old they are, in years
    */
  case class User(name: String, age: Int)

  /** What a user may do. */
  sealed trait Role

  /** Can do anything, invite others included. */
  case object Admin extends Role

  /** Can read and comment.
    *
    * @param team the team they're in
    */
  case class Member(team: String) extends Role

  /** Can look, for a while. */
  sealed trait Guest extends Role

  /** Invited by a member.
    *
    * @param by who invited them
    */
  case class Invited(by: String) extends Guest

  /** Came in by a shared link. */
  final class Anonymous extends Guest
}

import model._

val doc = Scaladoc.of[User].get
Scaladoc.of gives a type’s comment:
doc.textSummary
// res0: Option[String] = Some(value = "A user of the app.")
doc.textParams
// res1: Map[String, String] = Map(
//   "name" -> "the name shown to other users",
//   "age" -> "how old they are, in years"
// )
textSummary is the first sentence, the one scaladoc itself would show in a list of members. textParams is what each field means. A schema generator needs nothing more to give every property a title and a description.
Typedoc.of gives the comments of a type made of others, as a tree. Here each Some(doc) is shown as its text:
Typedoc.of[Role]
Sum: a sealed trait or class, with its variants, which can be sums themselves Product: a case class, with its fields, each documented by its @param Singleton: an object Plain: any other type
A Scala 3 enum is a Sum of its cases, a case with parameters a Product, and one without a Singleton:
/** How a user hears from the app. */
enum Channel {

  /** Not at all. */
  case Silent

  /** By e-mail.
    *
    * @param perDay how many a day, at most
    */
  case Email(perDay: Int)
}

Typedoc.of[Channel]
// Sum("Channel", "How a user hears from the app.", List(
//   Singleton("Silent", "Not at all."),
//   Product("Email", "By e-mail.", List(Field("perDay", "how many a day, at most")))))
A schema generator makes a sealed trait a oneOf, with a description for the whole and one for each alternative.

Apply the plugin next to Gradle’s scala plugin:
plugins {
    scala
    id("io.github.andyglow.scaladoc") version "0.6.0"
}
It reads the module’s Scala version from its scala3-library_3 or scala-library dependency and sets the compiler up for it: the compiler plugin on Scala 2, -Xread-docs on Scala 3 (-Yread-docs before 3.5). On Scala 2 it also adds scaladoc-annotation, and exposes it to the module’s consumers, as api would, published POMs included. As with sbt, it belongs on the modules that publish on Scala 2 and those that read on Scala 3. A scaladoc block holds the settings, which mean what the sbt plugin’s do:
scaladoc {
    excludeClasses.add("com.acme.**.internal.*")   // also includePaths, excludePaths and includeClasses
    version.set("0.6.0")                       // of the compiler plugin; the plugin's own by default
}
A module that reads scaladoc adds the parser itself:
dependencies {
    implementation("io.github.andyglow:scaladoc-parser_2.13:0.6.0")
}
The plugin runs on Gradle 8.5 and later.

A scala-cli project says what it needs in using directives, in any of its files, as in a project.scala.
A Scala 2 module that publishes its scaladoc needs the compiler plugin, and the annotation it embeds scaladoc in, which the module’s POM then brings to its consumers:
//> using plugin io.github.andyglow::scaladoc-compiler-plugin:0.6.0
//> using dep io.github.andyglow:scaladoc-annotation:0.6.0
The include and exclude patterns are the compiler plugin’s options, a using option each; see Choosing what to embed:
//> using option -P:scaladoc-compiler-plugin:exclude:class:com.acme.**.internal.*
A Scala 3 module that publishes needs nothing at all.
A module that reads scaladoc needs the parser, which brings scaladoc-core along:
//> using dep io.github.andyglow::scaladoc-parser:0.6.0
and on Scala 3, -Xread-docs as well:
//> using option -Xread-docs
This setup is tested with scala-cli on Scala 2.13, 2.12 and 3: a model is published locally, and read by an app and by a Scala 3 project that uses the 2.13 model. See modules/tests/scala-cli in the repository.

Before reaching for macros, it helps to see the parser on its own. Give it a comment, and it gives back what the comment says:
import scaladoc._

val parsed = Scaladoc.fromString(
  """/** Adds two numbers.
    |  *
    |  * @param a the first one
    |  * @param b the second one
    |  * @return their sum
    |  */""".stripMargin)
// parsed: Either[ScaladocException, Scaladoc] = Right(
//   value = Scaladoc(
//     tags = List(
//       Description(
//         markup = Document(
//           elements = List(
//             Paragraph(markup = List(PlainText(value = "Adds two numbers.")))
//           )
//         )
//       ),
//       Param(
//         name = "a",
//         markup = Document(
//           elements = List(
//             Paragraph(markup = List(PlainText(value = "the first one")))
//           )
//         )
//       ),
//       Param(
//         name = "b",
//         markup = Document(
//           elements = List(
//             Paragraph(markup = List(PlainText(value = "the second one")))
//           )
//         )
//       ),
//       Returns(
//         markup = Document(
//           elements = List(
//             Paragraph(markup = List(PlainText(value = "their sum")))
//           )
//         )
//       )
//     )
//   )
// )

parsed.map(_.textReturns)
// res0: Either[ScaladocException, Option[String]] = Right(
//   value = Some(value = "their sum")
// )
The result is an Either, because not every string is a doc comment. The Right holds a Scaladoc: a list of tags, each with its markup. The rest of this guide walks through that structure. Comment syntax covers what you can write, The model what you get back, and Scaladoc.of and Typedoc.of how to get it without having the comment as a string at all.

A doc comment starts with /** and ends with */. In between, each line usually starts with a * border: aligned under the second * of the opening marker in scaladoc’s style, under the first in javadoc’s. The border and one space after it belong to the comment’s frame, not to its text:
parse(
  """/** A comment in scaladoc's style,
    |  * the border under the second star.
    |  */""".stripMargin)
// res0: Markup = Document(
//   elements = List(
//     Paragraph(
//       markup = List(
//         PlainText(
//           value = """A comment in scaladoc's style,
// the border under the second star."""
//         )
//       )
//     )
//   )
// )

parse(
  """/**
    | * And one in javadoc's style.
    | */""".stripMargin)
// res1: Markup = Document(
//   elements = List(
//     Paragraph(markup = List(PlainText(value = "And one in javadoc's style.")))
//   )
// )
The border is optional, as it is for scaladoc. A line without one is text from its first character on:
parse(
  """/** No borders here,
    |    just text.
    | */""".stripMargin)
// res2: Markup = Document(
//   elements = List(
//     Paragraph(
//       markup = List(
//         PlainText(
//           value = """No borders here,
// just text."""
//         )
//       )
//     )
//   )
// )
Trailing whitespace and blank lines at the start and the end of a comment are dropped. What remains is text, and the text is always made of blocks: here, a single paragraph.

Inside a paragraph, a few markers change how text looks. They are scaladoc’s wiki markup:
Markup Model `code` Monospace ''italic'' Italic '''bold''' Bold __underline__ Underline ^super^ Superscript ,,sub,, Subscript [[link]] Link
Markup nests, so bold text can contain italic and links, and so on all the way down. The text of a monospace span is taken literally, which is what you want from code:
parse("/** '''Bold, with ''italic'' and [[scala.Option]] inside''', and `code with ''quotes''`. */")
// res4: Markup = Document(
//   elements = List(
//     Paragraph(
//       markup = List(
//         Bold(
//           spans = List(
//             PlainText(value = "Bold, with "),
//             Italic(spans = List(PlainText(value = "italic"))),
//             PlainText(value = " and "),
//             Link(target = "scala.Option", title = List()),
//             PlainText(value = " inside")
//           )
//         ),
//         PlainText(value = ", and "),
//         Monospace(value = "code with ''quotes''"),
//         PlainText(value = ".")
//       )
//     )
//   )
// )
Be conservative in what you do, be liberal in what you accept from others. — Jon Postel, RFC 761
Comments are written by people, and people write x^2 and forget a backtick. A marker that isn’t closed within its paragraph is therefore plain text, not an error that loses the whole comment:
parse("/** The cost grows like n^2, and `this backtick never closes. */")
// res5: Markup = Document(
//   elements = List(
//     Paragraph(
//       markup = List(
//         PlainText(
//           value = "The cost grows like n^2, and `this backtick never closes."
//         )
//       )
//     )
//   )
// )
HTML and javadoc tags are another matter: a <b> or a {@code that isn’t closed in its paragraph is an error, since nobody writes one by accident, and there’s no telling where it was meant to end. The error shows how the text goes from the tag:
Scaladoc.fromString("/** A <b>bold claim, never closed. */")
// res6: Either[ScaladocException, Scaladoc] = Left(
//   value = UnclosedMarkup(markup = "<b>", near = "<b>bold claim, never ...")
// )
Markup nests at most 64 deep. Deeper, a marker is text, as it is without a closer, and a tag is an error.

A link holds a target and, optionally, a title. The target runs to the first whitespace and the rest is the title, which can have markup of its own. The target is either an entity, like a class or a member, or a URL:
parse("/** See [[scala.Option]] and [[https://docs.scala-lang.org the ''Scala'' docs]]. */")
// res7: Markup = Document(
//   elements = List(
//     Paragraph(
//       markup = List(
//         PlainText(value = "See "),
//         Link(target = "scala.Option", title = List()),
//         PlainText(value = " and "),
//         Link(
//           target = "https://docs.scala-lang.org",
//           title = List(
//             PlainText(value = "the "),
//             Italic(spans = List(PlainText(value = "Scala"))),
//             PlainText(value = " docs")
//           )
//         ),
//         PlainText(value = ".")
//       )
//     )
//   )
// )
Member signatures can contain ]] themselves. A link opened with more brackets closes with as many, which keeps such a target in one piece:
parse("/** Overloads are told apart like [[[baz(x:List[List[A]])*]]]. */")
// res8: Markup = Document(
//   elements = List(
//     Paragraph(
//       markup = List(
//         PlainText(value = "Overloads are told apart like "),
//         Link(target = "baz(x:List[List[A]])*", title = List()),
//         PlainText(value = ".")
//       )
//     )
//   )
// )
Link.isUrl tells the two kinds of target apart.

A blank line ends a paragraph. A heading is a line of its own, wrapped in as many = on each side as its level, from one to six:
parse(
  """/** = The `App` trait =
    |  *
    |  * First paragraph,
    |  * still the first.
    |  *
    |  * == Caveats ==
    |  * Second paragraph.
    |  */""".stripMargin)
// res9: Markup = Document(
//   elements = List(
//     Heading(
//       level = One,
//       spans = List(
//         PlainText(value = "The "),
//         Monospace(value = "App"),
//         PlainText(value = " trait")
//       )
//     ),
//     Paragraph(
//       markup = List(
//         PlainText(
//           value = """First paragraph,
// still the first."""
//         )
//       )
//     ),
//     Heading(level = Two, spans = List(PlainText(value = "Caveats"))),
//     Paragraph(markup = List(PlainText(value = "Second paragraph.")))
//   )
// )
A heading doesn’t need a blank line before or after it, and its title can have markup too.

Code goes between {{{ and }}}. A code block can span blank lines, and loses the indentation that all of its lines share, so the code reads the same however deeply the comment is nested:
parse(
  """/** For example:
    |  * {{{
    |  *   val x = 1
    |  *
    |  *   if (x > 0)
    |  *     println(x)
    |  * }}}
    |  */""".stripMargin)
// res10: Markup = Document(
//   elements = List(
//     Paragraph(markup = List(PlainText(value = "For example:"))),
//     CodeBlock(
//       value = """val x = 1
// 
// if (x > 0)
//   println(x)""",
//       language = None
//     )
//   )
// )
Nothing inside a code block is a tag, so an example can show @main or @tailrec without the parser mistaking them for the start of a @main tag.

The list is the origin of culture. — Umberto Eco
A list item is an indented line that starts with - for a bullet, or with 1., i., I., a. or A. for the numbering styles. The indentation matters twice. Without it, a line that starts with - is just text. And more of it nests a list under the item before it:
parse(
  """/** Steps:
    |  *
    |  *   1. Clone the repository
    |  *   1. Build it
    |  *     - on the JVM
    |  *     - on Scala.js
    |  *   1. Run the tests
    |  */""".stripMargin)
// res11: Markup = Document(
//   elements = List(
//     Paragraph(markup = List(PlainText(value = "Steps:"))),
//     OrderedList(
//       items = List(
//         Document(
//           elements = List(
//             Paragraph(markup = List(PlainText(value = "Clone the repository")))
//           )
//         ),
//         Document(
//           elements = List(
//             Paragraph(markup = List(PlainText(value = "Build it"))),
//             UnorderedList(
//               items = List(
//                 Document(
//                   elements = List(
//                     Paragraph(markup = List(PlainText(value = "on the JVM")))
//                   )
//                 ),
//                 Document(
//                   elements = List(
//                     Paragraph(markup = List(PlainText(value = "on Scala.js")))
//                   )
//                 )
//               )
//             )
//           )
//         ),
//         Document(
//           elements = List(
//             Paragraph(markup = List(PlainText(value = "Run the tests")))
//           )
//         )
//       ),
//       style = Decimal
//     )
//   )
// )
Each item is a document of its own, holding the item’s text and the lists nested under it. An item’s text continues on the lines that follow, until the next item, a blank line or the end of the list. A blank line between items doesn’t end the list. A different numbering style at the same indentation starts a new list.

Tables use pipes, a header row, and a delimiter row that sets each column’s alignment with ::
parse(
  """/** Sizes:
    |  *
    |  * | Size | Price | Notes      |
    |  * |:-----|------:|:----------:|
    |  * | S    | 10    | ''cheap''  |
    |  * | M    | 15    |
    |  */""".stripMargin)
// res12: Markup = Document(
//   elements = List(
//     Paragraph(markup = List(PlainText(value = "Sizes:"))),
//     Table(
//       header = Row(
//         cells = List(
//           Paragraph(markup = List(PlainText(value = "Size"))),
//           Paragraph(markup = List(PlainText(value = "Price"))),
//           Paragraph(markup = List(PlainText(value = "Notes")))
//         )
//       ),
//       alignments = List(Left, Right, Center),
//       rows = List(
//         Row(
//           cells = List(
//             Paragraph(markup = List(PlainText(value = "S"))),
//             Paragraph(markup = List(PlainText(value = "10"))),
//             Paragraph(
//               markup = List(Italic(spans = List(PlainText(value = "cheap"))))
//             )
//           )
//         ),
//         Row(
//           cells = List(
//             Paragraph(markup = List(PlainText(value = "M"))),
//             Paragraph(markup = List(PlainText(value = "15"))),
//             Paragraph(markup = List())
//           )
//         )
//       )
//     )
//   )
// )
Cells hold inline markup, and \| is a pipe inside a cell. Rows are padded or cut to the width of the header. Lines without a valid delimiter row aren’t a table at all, and stay text.

Four or more dashes on a line of their own make a rule:
parse("/** Above.\n  *\n  * ----\n  *\n  * Below.\n  */")
// res13: Markup = Document(
//   elements = List(
//     Paragraph(markup = List(PlainText(value = "Above."))),
//     HorizontalRule,
//     Paragraph(markup = List(PlainText(value = "Below.")))
//   )
// )
As in scaladoc, a rule starts a block, but doesn’t interrupt a paragraph: ---- right under a line of text is text.

Doc comments have always had HTML in them, often from the days when they were written for javadoc. The parser turns it into the same model as the wiki markup, wherever the model has an equivalent:
<p> and <div> break paragraphs, <h1> to <h6> are headings, <li> are list items, <pre> is a code block <b> and <strong> are bold, <i> and <em> italic, <u> underlined, <sup> and <sub> raised and lowered <code> and <tt> are monospace, <a href="..."> is a link, <br> is a line break entities like &lt;, &#60; and &nbsp; are the characters they stand for <!-- comments --> go, with any tags in them
parse("/** <p>Returns a <code>List&lt;A&gt;</code> of <b>all</b> the <a href=\"https://example.com\">items</a>.</p> */")
// res14: Markup = Document(
//   elements = List(
//     Paragraph(
//       markup = List(
//         PlainText(value = "Returns a "),
//         Monospace(value = "List<A>"),
//         PlainText(value = " of "),
//         Bold(spans = List(PlainText(value = "all"))),
//         PlainText(value = " the "),
//         Link(
//           target = "https://example.com",
//           title = List(PlainText(value = "items"))
//         ),
//         PlainText(value = ".")
//       )
//     )
//   )
// )
The tags that become the model, from <b> to <a href="...">, have to be closed in their paragraph (see Inline markup). Other tags that scaladoc keeps, like <span> or <table>, come back as HtmlTag spans with the tag as written, closed or not. A < that isn’t a tag at all, as in List<String>, is text. HTML inside {{{ code blocks stays as it is, and so it does in <pre>, except that <code> tags go and entities are decoded.

Javadoc’s inline tags are understood too:
Tag Model {@code x} Monospace {@value x} Monospace {@link X}, {@link X label} Link, with the label as its title {@linkplain X}, {@linkplain X label} Link, with the label as its title {@literal x} text {@docRoot} nothing
parse("/** Wraps a {@link java.util.Map} of {@code Map<K, {V}>}, see {@linkplain Foo#bar the bar}. */")
// res15: Markup = Document(
//   elements = List(
//     Paragraph(
//       markup = List(
//         PlainText(value = "Wraps a "),
//         Link(target = "java.util.Map", title = List()),
//         PlainText(value = " of "),
//         Monospace(value = "Map<K, {V}>"),
//         PlainText(value = ", see "),
//         Link(target = "Foo#bar", title = List(PlainText(value = "the bar"))),
//         PlainText(value = ".")
//       )
//     )
//   )
// )
Braces inside a tag must balance, which is what lets {@code Map<K, {V}>} hold braces of its own, and a tag has to be closed in its paragraph, in Markdown too. Others, like {@inheritDoc}, are text.

Simplicity is prerequisite for reliability. — Edsger W. Dijkstra
Scala 3’s scaladoc reads doc comments as Markdown by default, and wiki syntax only when told to. The parser reads both, and is told which one a comment is written in, since the same characters mean different things in the two: __x__ is underline in wiki and bold in Markdown, and =x= is a heading in one and text in the other.
Scaladoc.fromString and fromDocstring take the syntax as a parameter, wiki by default. A comment can also say it for itself, as in Scala 3’s scaladoc, with @syntax wiki or @syntax markdown on a line of its own; the tag isn’t kept in the parsed comment. Scaladoc.of picks the syntax by where it finds the comment.
parseMarkdown(
  """/** Some **bold**, `code` and a [link](https://scala-lang.org).
    |  *
    |  * - [x] a task, done
    |  * - [ ] and one to do
    |  */""".stripMargin)
// res17: Markup = Document(
//   elements = List(
//     Paragraph(
//       markup = List(
//         PlainText(value = "Some "),
//         Bold(spans = List(PlainText(value = "bold"))),
//         PlainText(value = ", "),
//         Monospace(value = "code"),
//         PlainText(value = " and a "),
//         Link(
//           target = "https://scala-lang.org",
//           title = List(PlainText(value = "link"))
//         ),
//         PlainText(value = ".")
//       )
//     ),
//     UnorderedList(
//       items = List(
//         Document(
//           elements = List(
//             Paragraph(
//               markup = List(
//                 Checkbox(checked = true),
//                 PlainText(value = " a task, done")
//               )
//             )
//           )
//         ),
//         Document(
//           elements = List(
//             Paragraph(
//               markup = List(
//                 Checkbox(checked = false),
//                 PlainText(value = " and one to do")
//               )
//             )
//           )
//         )
//       )
//     )
//   )
// )

Scaladoc.fromString(
  """/** Some '''bold''' text.
    |  *
    |  * @syntax wiki
    |  */""".stripMargin, syntax = CommentSyntax.Markdown).map(_.descriptions)
// res18: Either[ScaladocException, Seq[Markup]] = Right(
//   value = List(
//     Document(
//       elements = List(
//         Paragraph(
//           markup = List(
//             PlainText(value = "Some "),
//             Bold(spans = List(PlainText(value = "bold"))),
//             PlainText(value = " text.")
//           )
//         )
//       )
//     )
//   )
// )
Both syntaxes give the same model, named for what the markup means, so tools that walk it work the same on either: '''bold''' and **bold** are both Bold. Tags work the same in both, and only their text’s markup differs. A few nodes only come from Markdown, because wiki syntax can’t write them: BlockQuote, Strikethrough, Image, LineBreak, Checkbox and footnotes; see The model.

The Markdown is Scala 3’s: CommonMark, with the extensions Scala 3’s scaladoc turns on.
Blocks: paragraphs, # and underlined headings, fenced (```, ~~~, with a language) and indented code, block quotes, bullet and numbered lists, thematic breaks, and HTML blocks. Inlines: *emphasis* and **strong** (with _ too), code spans, links ([text](url) and by reference), images, entities and escapes, and hard line breaks after a \. Scala 3’s extensions: tables, task lists, ~~strikethrough~~, bare URLs and email addresses as links, footnotes ([^1]), and scaladoc’s own [[links]] and {{{ code }}}. Javadoc’s inline tags, as in wiki: {@code x}, {@link x} and the rest.
HTML is cleaned first, as Scala 3’s scaladoc does it: the tags it keeps stay (<b>, <code>, <span>, <table> and the like), <p> and <div> are paragraph breaks, and other tags go. As in Scala 3, that includes what only looks like a tag, <https://x.y> or the <A> in List<A>:
parseMarkdown("/** A <b>safe</b> tag, a <font>gone</font> one, and List<A> or <https://x.y> */")
// res19: Markup = Document(
//   elements = List(
//     Paragraph(
//       markup = List(
//         PlainText(value = "A "),
//         HtmlTag(data = "<b>"),
//         PlainText(value = "safe"),
//         HtmlTag(data = "</b>"),
//         PlainText(value = " tag, a gone one, and List or")
//       )
//     )
//   )
// )

Omit needless words. — William Strunk Jr., The Elements of Style
Many tools want a string, not a tree: a schema needs a description, a log line needs a message. Two methods give one. plainString writes the markup back in wiki syntax, and trimmed tidies the whitespace first, which is usually what you want:
val m = Scaladoc.fromString("/** A   ''nice''\n  *   description. */").map(_.descriptions.head)
// m: Either[ScaladocException, Markup] = Right(
//   value = Document(
//     elements = List(
//       Paragraph(
//         markup = List(
//           PlainText(value = "A   "),
//           Italic(spans = List(PlainText(value = "nice"))),
//           PlainText(
//             value = """
//   description."""
//           )
//         )
//       )
//     )
//   )
// )

m.map(_.plainString)
// res2: Either[ScaladocException, String] = Right(
//   value = """A   ''nice''
//   description."""
// )
m.map(_.trimmed.plainString)
// res3: Either[ScaladocException, String] = Right(
//   value = "A ''nice'' description."
// )
Scaladoc has helpers for the parts that tools ask for most. Each comes in two flavors: one that returns the markup, and a text one that returns it as trimmed plain text.
Markup Text What descriptions textDescriptions the description params textParams @params by name typeParams textTypeParams @tparams by name throws textThrows @throws by exception type returns textReturns @return summary textSummary the first sentence, as scaladoc shows it in member lists
The summary is the description’s first paragraph up to and with its first full stop, the one followed by a space or the end. A @shortDescription tag replaces it:
Scaladoc.fromString(
  """/** Keeps the users of the app. All of them,
    |  * in one place.
    |  */""".stripMargin).map(_.textSummary)
// res4: Either[ScaladocException, Option[String]] = Right(
//   value = Some(value = "Keeps the users of the app.")
// )

Any sufficiently advanced technology is indistinguishable from magic. — Arthur C. Clarke, Profiles of the Future
Scaladoc.of[T] looks like it shouldn’t work. It’s an ordinary expression, and yet it returns the doc comment written above T’s definition, which is something the running program never had. The trick is that it doesn’t run at runtime at all. It’s a macro: it runs inside the compiler, which does know the comment, parses it there, and leaves the finished model behind in your code as a constant.
import scaladoc._

object geometry {

  /** A point on a plane.
    *
    * @param x the horizontal coordinate
    * @param y the vertical one
    */
  case class Point(x: Double, y: Double)
}

Scaladoc.of[geometry.Point].map(_.textParams)
// res0: Option[Map[String, String]] = Some(
//   value = Map("x" -> "the horizontal coordinate", "y" -> "the vertical one")
// )
That makes it a natural building block for derivation. A macro or a type class instance that describes T can call Scaladoc.of[T] and get titles and descriptions for free. A type without a comment gives None.
A type made of others, a sealed hierarchy or an enum, has a comment on each part. Typedoc.of[T] is the same trick for those, and gives them all, as a tree:
object shapes {

  /** A shape. */
  sealed trait Shape

  /** A circle.
    *
    * @param radius the radius
    */
  case class Circle(radius: Double) extends Shape

  /** No shape at all. */
  case object Empty extends Shape

  /** A shape with straight sides. */
  sealed trait Polygon extends Shape

  /** A square.
    *
    * @param side the length of a side
    */
  case class Square(side: Double) extends Polygon

  /** Drawn by hand. */
  final class Freehand extends Polygon
}
Here each Some(doc) is shown as its text:
Typedoc.of[shapes.Shape]
A Scala 3 enum is a Sum of its cases, a case with parameters a Product, and one without a Singleton:
/** A traffic light's signal. */
enum Signal {

  /** Stop. */
  case Red

  /** Go, after a pause.
    *
    * @param seconds how long the pause is
    */
  case Green(seconds: Int)
}

Typedoc.of[Signal]
// Sum("Signal", "A traffic light's signal.", List(
//   Singleton("Red", "Stop."),
//   Product("Green", "Go, after a pause.", List(Field("seconds", "how long the pause is")))))
A derivation that describes a sealed trait as a oneOf, say, gets the description of the whole and of each alternative from it. The section on the tree, below, has the details.
What remains is the question of where the compiler finds the comments, which is the same for both, and the answer is different on Scala 2 and on Scala 3.

When you have the comment as text, there’s no need for a macro. Scaladoc.fromString parses it, in wiki syntax unless told otherwise, with syntax = CommentSyntax.Markdown, or unless the comment says otherwise:
Scaladoc.fromString("/** Hello. */").map(_.textDescriptions)
// res3: Either[ScaladocException, Seq[String]] = Right(value = List("Hello."))
Scala 3’s Symbol.docstring returns a comment with one quirk: the indentation of its first line is gone, while the other lines keep theirs. Scaladoc.fromDocstring parses that form. With strict = true, it puts the indentation back and checks the border’s alignment, exactly as Scaladoc.of does:
Scaladoc.fromDocstring("/** Nested class.\n    *\n    * @param a the a\n    */", strict = true).map(_.textParams)
// res4: Either[ScaladocException, Map[String, String]] = Right(
//   value = Map("a" -> "the a")
// )
